To fix the animation that automatically plays for the collapsed navigation bar on page load, you need to understand both the CSS and JavaScript causing it.

### **The Cause**

1. **CSS Rules**: In [frontend/css/navigation.css](frontend/css/navigation.css), the `#navigation-bar` element has a persistent CSS transition:
   [frontend/css/navigation.css](frontend/css/navigation.css#L15): `transition: width 0.3s ease;`
   Similarly, `.nav-logo-text` and `.nav-text` respectively have transitions applied to them for opacity:
   [frontend/css/navigation.css](frontend/css/navigation.css#L43): `transition: opacity 0.3s ease;`
   [frontend/css/navigation.css](frontend/css/navigation.css#L113): `transition: opacity 0.3s ease;`
2. **JavaScript Logic**: When the page loads, [frontend/js/navigation.js](frontend/js/navigation.js#L23-L25) checks `localStorage` for the saved navigation state. If it was closed, it dynamically appends the `.collapsed` class to the `#navigation-bar` and `document.body` *after* the raw uncollapsed styles have already initialized visually. 

Because the transition styles are perpetually active on these elements, the JavaScript dynamically applying the `.collapsed` class on load triggers the CSS engine to visually animate the shrinking effect every time the user navigates or refreshes.

### **How to Restrict the Animation**

To ensure the navigation bar transitions **only** occur when a user explicitly clicks the toggle button, you should suppress CSS transitions during the initial page load.

**Step 1: Create a CSS class to disable transitions entirely**
In your [frontend/css/main.css](frontend/css/main.css) or the top of [frontend/css/navigation.css](frontend/css/navigation.css), add a class that disables transitions:

```css
.no-transition,
.no-transition * {
    transition: none !important;
}
```

**Step 2: Add this class prior to initialization and remove it afterward**
By default, the body should start with the `.no-transition` class (you can add this directly to your `body` tag in the HTML files like `<body class="no-transition">`). Then modify the JS logic handling the initialization in [frontend/js/navigation.js](frontend/js/navigation.js#L23) so it waits briefly before removing the suppression lock:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    // Determine nav bar and state...

    // Check local storage and apply the initial .collapsed state BEFORE transitions are turned on
    if (localStorage.getItem('nav_collapsed') === 'true') {
        navBar.classList.add('collapsed');
        document.body.classList.add('nav-collapsed');
    }

    // Force a browser reflow so the layout updates immediately without animating
    void navBar.offsetHeight; 

    // Remove the lock so future clicks will successfully animate
    document.body.classList.remove('no-transition');

    /* ... rest of your code ... */
});
```

Using this approach, the layout instantly snaps to the `.collapsed` dimensions on loading and only physically animates later when the `toggleNavigation` listener executes.

